 @Orcawhale1  Correction: Lisa pulled away most of Raja's team from Vega to Navi. They got a much-needed shot in the arm from Sony to kick Navi's development into high gear, so Lisa poured most of Radeon's resources into that. She didn't pull from GPU to CPU, but from one GPU architecture to another.

And no, Navi/RDNA was not complete before Zen. It was still in its finalization stages in 2018, with another team already well into development on RDNA 2. They are beginning to give their GPUs the same treatment they give their CPUs, where the teams will leapfrog one another so there are always incremental improvements from one iteration to the next. But even if that were not the case, Zen was finished and already shipped to the public in the form of the Ryzen 2000 series (Zen+) before Navi (RDNA) was finalized.

Also, Jim Keller isn't the only one responsible for Zen. Not by a long shot. Zen's chief architect is actually Michael Clark, who's still with the company. Sure, Jim helped and is good at what he does, but he does not deserve all (or even most) of the credit. And even less so now, now that they've moved on to Zen+, Zen 2, and then also Zen 3 this year, none of which Keller had anything to do with. In fact, while he was helping with Zen, Keller was also developing a Zen-based ARM CPU for AMD before he left... again.

